/*
RMSTEST_40b - elevation GROWTH bias, per-clump budget = 25 tiles
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 11 (Sec.6.2's model)

READ THIS ON WHICHEVER OF 40a/40b/40c YOU OPEN FIRST - the three are one
experiment and only the per-clump budget differs.

WHAT BATCH 6 OVERTURNED
RMSTEST_39 measured the elevation SEED distribution cleanly for the first time
(100 clumps at the 6-tile floor, ~94 components from a declared 100, so each
component is one seed). Two results killed Sec.6.2's model:

  - the SEED bias is only 1.31:1, not the 18:1 or 29:1 taken from grown tiles
  - the seed bias is FLAT against distance from the map edge (1.4/1.3/1.5/1.2/
    1.2/1.5), so the "border band" is not a seeding property at all

And the grown-tile ratio decomposes exactly as seed ratio x clump-size ratio
(1.31 x 1.41 = 1.86 against a measured 1.88; 1.78 x 4.03 = 7.18 against 7.16).
**The dominant term is clump SIZE, not seed placement.** Whatever produces the
diagonal bias in a finished map happens during GROWTH.

Worse for the old model, the observed ratio tracked how much elevation the
script asked for - 1.88:1 at 1.55% coverage, 7.16:1 at 6.21%, 12.86:1 at 21.47%
- on otherwise identical scripts. So the "step with weight 18 vs 1" was never an
engine constant; it was an emergent statistic of one script's coverage.

WHAT THIS TRIO MEASURES
Hold the clump COUNT fixed at 50 and sweep the per-clump BUDGET, which is the
amount of growth each clump does:

  40a   50 clumps x   6 tiles =  300   (0.75% coverage) - the measured floor,
                                        i.e. almost no discretionary growth
  40b   50 clumps x  25 tiles = 1250   (3.1%)
  40c   50 clumps x 100 tiles = 5000   (12.5%)

Fifty clumps is deliberately few. RMSTEST_35 declared 500 and returned ~283
components, so nearly half had merged and its "clump size" was really merged-
component size - the confound this design exists to avoid. At 50 clumps merging
should be rare even at 40c's coverage, so component size IS clump size.

READ IT WITH
  Connected components of elevation > 0.
  Per run: component count against the declared 50; the favoured:disfavoured
  split of component CENTROIDS (the seed ratio); the mean component SIZE on each
  side (the growth ratio); and the raw TILE ratio.
  The identity tile_ratio = seed_ratio x size_ratio should hold in each - if it
  does not, components are merging and the run needs fewer clumps.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. SEED ratio stays near 1.3:1 at all three budgets. It is a property of seed
   placement and nothing here changes that. If it moves with budget, seeding and
   growth are not separable and the decomposition above is wrong.
2. SIZE ratio RISES with the budget - near 1.0 at 40a (no growth to be biased)
   climbing at 40b and 40c. That is the signature of a directional growth
   preference and is the whole point of the trio. **The rate at which it rises
   is the constant Sec.6.2 needs**, in place of the fitted 18:1.
3. If the SIZE ratio is flat across all three, growth is unbiased, and the
   coverage dependence found in batch 6 comes from something else - crowding, or
   the budget interacting with placement - and Sec.6.2 needs a different model
   again. That is the expensive outcome and it is why 40a is included: it is the
   near-zero-growth control.
4. Component count should stay near 50. Well below ~45 at 40c means merging is
   material there and only 40a/40b can be read for size.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH ANY PLAYER COUNT - no player lands, so the count
does not enter. RUN EACH OF THE THREE THREE TIMES (nine generations total), and
REGENERATE BETWEEN SAVES - four separate batches have now produced duplicate
exports, and the check is that the elevated-tile total differs run to run.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  create_elevation 1
  {
    number_of_clumps 50
    number_of_tiles 1250
    spacing 1
  }

/* NO <CLIFF_GENERATION> HEADER - an empty one generates cliffs. */

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
